        author = "This catalogue, as a proposal (not an established model)",
        year = 2026,
        summary = "A mixed scheme for care at home and in homes: employers and workers share a contribution, general taxes cover the rest, and public standards govern providers, with limits on how many homes one owner may hold and published staffing and death rates.",
        details = "Keeps: shared employer and worker contributions, as in Germany's long-term care insurance, from 1995 (Social market economy); public funding that a view credits with easing family strain, as in Japan's long-term care insurance, from 2000 (Keynesian welfare capitalism). Blocks: private owners with few checks, which Private equity ownership of nursing homes links to higher mortality (Monetarist neoliberalism, Classical laissez-faire capitalism); safeguard: public approval of ownership changes and published mortality. One owner dominating a region (Social market economy); safeguard: a cap on homes per owner. Staff shortage and rising public costs, flagged in Japan's scheme (Modern monetary theory); safeguard: a published staffing ratio and yearly cost review. Test: over several years from launch, compare 90-day mortality after admission, preventable emergency visits and carers' strain between approved and other homes. Source: vdek, history of long-term care insurance; Makiko Takao, 2017 Japanese-language article on the long-term care insurance scheme (secondary); Gupta and colleagues, NBER Working Paper 28474, summarised in an NBER digest (2021); Müller-Armack, Wirtschaftslenkung und Marktwirtschaft (1947); Kelton, The Deficit Myth (2020).",
    ),
)
